.TH DATE 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
date \- print the current date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
.RI [+ format ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print the current date and time in UTC. With no argument, prints a fixed default format (e.g.
.IR "Sun Sep 20 14:05:09 UTC 2026" ).
With a
.RI + format
argument, renders that format instead, using
.BR strftime (3)-style
conversion specifiers:
.TP
.B %Y
4-digit year
.TP
.B %m
2-digit month (01-12)
.TP
.B %d
2-digit day of month (01-31)
.TP
.B %H %M %S
2-digit hour, minute, second
.TP
.B %j
3-digit day of year (001-366)
.TP
.B %A %a
full / abbreviated weekday name
.TP
.B %B %b
full / abbreviated month name
.TP
.B %s
seconds since the Unix epoch
.TP
.B %n %t %%
newline, tab, a literal percent sign
.SH NOTES
UTC only: there is no
.B $TZ
or local-timezone support yet. Read-only: there is no
.BR "\-s/\-\-set"
to change the system clock yet. Both are documented gaps, not silent bugs -- see mitos\-utils\-compat(7).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
